#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088865 :tutorial/content "Consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/a2/a2660977b21e476e1a295e215ac22888625763b12b1685521c289adf8326f78c.png)\n\nNo matter which number $x$ we pick as an input, $f(x)$ always will return an output. Therefore, we can write the domain of this function as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nshown below:\n\n![](images/84/84fefed3d54e57467ca3ebcf823c6bbf5c3c6aacce2f7542230b1abacfd97f46.png)\n\nWe cannot substitute the value $x=0$ into the function $g(x)$ because division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not part of the domain of $g(x)$. However, we can use any other number $x$ as input for $g(x)$. Therefore, the domain of $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty, 0)\\cup (0,\\infty)\n$$\n\nThis notation means that any number *apart from zero* can be used as an input for $g(x)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "e9b55c3b-309c-593d-bfdc-2164ed4cf232" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1489 :tutorial/title "The Domain of a Function Given as a Graph"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088866 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the function $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/2a/2a666cbc79869f13f7079db92396ae6f7ad2b2041b2aa0ea704149119a850eed.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function is not defined for $x<0$, nor is it defined for $x > 2$.\n- The function has filled circles at $x=0$ and $x=2$, so it is defined at both of these points.\n- In addition, the function is defined for every value of $x$ between $0$ and $2$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nWe can also express the domain using interval notation:\n\n$$\nx\\in [0,2]\n$$\n\nThe square brackets $[\\:]$ indicate that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ are included in the domain." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7454c76-12f4-56da-b55f-0fcbaed1c9f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1490 :tutorial/title "Functions Defined on Closed Intervals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088981 :tutorial/content "The **domain** of a function is the set of all input values for which it is defined.\n\nFor example, consider the function represented by the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7c39c6cdd67f1b69248b38e009b6de12acef250a59543aca2d2922ec1801d57.png)\n\nThe domain corresponds to the set of values in the \"Input\" set. Therefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider a situation where a function is defined using a table." :tutorial/id #uuid "e8b5c9de-7970-5fcf-b242-aa035f6c2492" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5909 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088982 :tutorial/content "Now consider the function $y=g(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c99d3cbb1b7ba516cc2cf75856aeb17b9f91d23185d6fd25319a492ca4e3b164.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function is not defined for $x<0$, nor is it defined for $x > 2$.\n- The function has open circles at $x=0$ and $x=2$, so it is *not* defined at either of these points.\n- However, the function is defined for every value of $x$ between $0$ and $2$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n0 \\lt x \\lt 2\n$$\n\nwhich we can write using interval notation as\n\n$$\nx\\in (0,2)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\:)$ indicate that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ are excluded from the domain." :tutorial/id #uuid "f49de54b-2bd4-5ee3-9e87-ca07573dea27" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5919 :tutorial/title "Functions Defined on Open Intervals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088983 :tutorial/content "It's possible for a function to have one endpoint included in the domain and another endpoint excluded.\n\nFor example, consider the function is $y=h(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0919c9b416ad49ed08d566b8c85d250a1a00a3ebf72f4d22c3d608fb42ede169.png)\n\nThe endpoint $x=-2$ is included in the domain, but the endpoint $x=2$ is excluded. Therefore, the domain of $h(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq x \\lt 2\n$$\n\nwhich we can write using interval notation as\n\n$$\nx\\in[-2, 2)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "85e2582d-5cd6-558b-8925-e538db397f3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5920 :tutorial/title "Functions Defined on Semi-Open Intervals"}]}
